\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\f0\fs24\cf0
\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 There are people who believe, against all sense, that fine art is a sign of higher culture. They hang paintings on walls as if venerating gods, as though art has something to do with the truth. But these people are idiots. Art has nothing to do with the truth. Art is seduction. An artist is seduced by themselves, and through their art, they seduce others.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92re arrogant.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Everyone thinks they can see the essential features of the world more clearly than everyone else, but only an artist has the arrogance to make a poem or painting to try and prove it. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I paint what I see,\u8221\'94 they say, or worse: \u8220\'93I write what I know\u8221\'94. As if anyone knows anything. As if it were possible to capture the magnitude of a thing like beauty by piling paint upon itself. And this gets to the truth: everything is connected to everything, so to capture a single thing, one must cut it out of the web in which it sits, and that kills it. And so every painting is carcass. Every poem is a trail of organs. And more: societies which value the arts are death cults.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri burped.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took a deep swig of his burgeon hooch and let the drink carry his thoughts further into this hole, because the everything-is-connected-to-everything angle neatly explained why every artist who has ever tried to capture the beauty of nature had failed.  Some paintings were nice to look at, sure, but the greatest painting in all the world was a mere smear of color compared the patchwork of hillfarms spread out before him. Each fenced-in farm was a swatch of chalky blue-green to pink, courtesy of the saturating evening sky. And the level of detail was boundless, and he was inside of it in a way that one could never be inside of a painting.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 But that goes without saying.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And as it goes, he took another drink. Then he took a more determined drink, because he was beginning to see profoundity in the most obvious things, and that was troubling. He knew well that profundity was a trick of the midwit and the philosopher, death to them all.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Can\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t be inside of a painting?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Obviously.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took another big swig of his hooch and let the warm beauty of the pinkish clouds work their magic on him. These were }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 tall, mountainous clouds, the kind that swallowed landscape paintings whole, the kind that cast broad shadows down across the world to pull the viewer through the distance, as they pulled him now, all the way out to the sparkling Shalbrum sea. And the sun\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s s}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 parkle on the sea\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s surface was blinding. That was a thing about the high from these down-valley burgeons\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 they could make textures slide and edges bleed, but the chief psychoactive effect was that anything bright would sparkle: the sunlit cloudrims, the seashine, every bright line and specular reflection would tighten up and sparkle like a twinkling star.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The stars themselves?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 In a couple hands, when the sun was gone, and the night had come, and the burgeon binge had settled his mind\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s eye into a dumbstruck gaze, all the pinpricked stars would bleed their white into the night\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 bright streams dripping down into the sea. Then, his sight would swirl everything together into one solid, flexing sheet that pulsed and breathed like an animal.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He licked his lips, then tossed his emptied hooch skin into his handcart.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He rummaged around the cart, looking for the other skin of hooch that he knew was hiding under the mess of rolled up paintings. His hands trembled as they searched for it, because it was his last skin of burgeon hooch, and his nerves knew it. By midday tomorrow, he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d be all out.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Then\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And then\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Then he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d be bricked into sobriety.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And it wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t even good hooch! More sugar water than hooch. It looked like milk. And he didn\u8217\'92t like the taste}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 . Down valley burgeons have waxy capsule leaves that split too easy, meaning the insides of the bulb dried out before he could get to them in the ways that he wanted to. The only way to have them was to spike a hooch.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He eventually found the fat skin of hooch nesting in between two twine-wrapped jars of powdered pigment. He pulled the skin out, popped the cork and took a swig. Then he took a longer swig and continued spiraling over the fact that he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d ever picked up a paintbrush.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Because what was a painting, really?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Paint that tree\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 that tall, stout, tree whose bushy head leaned over this dusty country lane. If he were to paint that tree, he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d be forced to reduce all its leaves to flecks of evening green. A living leaf with all its complexity, its pattern, its anastomosing veins pumped full of vital fluids, its lime-bright birth and brown death, its fall from the branch, its decay into dirt, that whole nested universe, reduced to a fleck of shadow in the evening. Reduced, because again, art is about seduction. A painting hides everything the artist doesn\u8217\'92t want the audience to look at. Paint an oasis, and look, there\u8217\'92s a naked woman bathing in the mossy water. Focus the viewer\u8217\'92s gaze by sharpening the droplets of jade that gleam on her cinnamon skin. The leaf? Feather its edge into the sky. Blame it on the effects of atmospheric haze. Better yet, don\u8217\'92t paint the leaf, paint bunches of leafy shapes! If the composition would be improved by removing the tree entirely, then it would almost be }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 wrong}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  to not to do so.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Absurd.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The arrogance! }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 What kind of person sees the wonder of the world and thinks, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93People ought to know about }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 my}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  interpretation\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94? As if the god of all creation was not already speaking. And somehow other people encourage this delusion. Crowds of enablers herd themselves into art galleries to be seduced. The immeasurable wonder of the wider world is right outside, but they\u8217\'92d all rather see it cornered, killed, flayed, with choice sections of its hide stitched into pretty frames, then put on display. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Yes, civilization is a death cult.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Go and paint all this,\u8221\'94 Mandri said to the mosquitoes as he swept his arm over the sun. \u8220\'93It\u8217\'92s not possible. Take as long as you like. Take a lifetime. Take ten lifetimes! Then\u8230\'85 and then\u8230\'85\u8221\'94 And then he forgot what he was on about because a scarecrow in the nearby field turned and looked at him.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It was not the first time Mandri had seen a scarecrow come to life. Another affect of the more powerful strains of burgeons was that they could, on rare occasion, breathe life into the inanimate. Stone statues of gods and heroes might turn their heads, blink, or even speak. A wood block scarecrow once told Mandri to \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93go away!\u8221\'94. This particular scarecrow eyed him through the shadow of its hood, seemingly daring him to try and pluck a bean pod from the many bean poles which , the gentle hill, a hill impaled with so many bean poles that it looked a bit like the hill had picked a fight with a porcupine. Basag beans is what they were, named after Basag, the pilgrim who\u8217\'92d been tied to a pole by demons.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Hullo!\u8221\'94 the scarecrow said in a stern tone, as if to say, \u8220\'93don\u8217\'92t think I don\u8217\'92t know what you\u8217\'92re up to.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 But this scarecrow did not know what Mandri was up to. Mandri was not looking for beans. He was looking for paintings. And there were plenty of paintings scattered about the Hrkan world, even out here. Most country folk were poor, but through the magic of inheritance, any given farmhouse might have a masterwork hanging above the fireplace. And that was the thrill of the job. Licensed vagrancy had very few perks, but being ambushed by a work of art in its natural environment was one of them. Because yes, art was useless, frilly, and the march of civilization had sterilized the craft with repitition and pattern and formula, so much so that half the paintings in Mandri\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s cart weren\u8217\'92t actually paintings, but lithograph prints with a few added brushstrokes of brighter color to help sell the illusion of effort\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 but!\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 as decoration, as an ornament which defines a home and the vibe of a place, as that-thing-on-the-wall which kids grow up with, a work of art has some value.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The scarecrow folded its arms.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Now that was something a burgeon could not do. Full body movement.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Then the scarecrow , and Mandri was forced to conclude that this was not a scarecrow at all, but an old man. A farmer. Exactly the kind of creature Mandri was want to see. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Hello!\u8221\'94 Mandri shouted. He waved, trying to appear cheery. If he were lucky, then this old man had outlived his wife. That meant that she wouldn\u8217\'92t be around to stop her husband from selling her treasured inheritance.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Ya lost?\u8221\'94 the farmer shouted.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Never!\u8221\'94 Mandri replied. \u8220\'93}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I peddle paints and paintings and p. I can perform restorations, if desired. I can give you views from the Sand\u8217\'92s eight portions. Women, tasteful and lewd. A god to firm up your walls, or give luck for the harvest. All of it, at every price.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The gods are all dead,\u8221\'94 the old farmer sneered.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93That may be,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, \u8220\'93But I have a mask of Solraheen here which looks very much alive in the eyes, and I don\u8217\'92t know, maybe He is. And perhaps you have some paintings you\u8217\'92d like to sell?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93All I got is a knife wheel and a fish boat that\u8217\'92s sunk in the }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri ambled of the country lane, turning his handcart with some difficulty because he had to lift the damn thing up to turn it. Broken swing gate.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t bother!\u8221\'94 the old farmer called. \u8220\'93Whatever ya sellin\u8217\'92, I ain\u8217\'92t buyin\u8217\'92!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Licensed vagrancy required tact and guile, as well as willingness to deceive. He lacked a natural these virtues. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  It was an ox. It was the same damned yellow-headed ox from Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s leech farm.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 What were the chances?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 This far out?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mruuuuu,\u8221\'94 the ox said when it noticed him gawking. It sauntered through burgeon addled haze, dewlap flapping. The skip in its step suggested it recognized him. And as it came into focus, the state of its distress became all too clear: red scrapes all over, courtesy of the thorns surrounding Vigram\u8217\'92s farm. Purple bruises splotched its armpits. The rest of its shaven body was a grotesque mosaic of little blood rings where leeches used to be. Those leeches still attached were concentrated on its haunches, with a few suckling on its belly.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The poor thing was obviously unable to care for itself, and so it was coming to him for help. It had no idea where it was. Absent a miracle, it was going to die.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s heart went out to the creature.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I can\u8217\'92t do anything for you,\u8221\'94 he said, but even as he said it, he knew it wasn\u8217\'92t true. He could do something. He could scrape off those leeches. He could lead the beast to water. He could go to the extreme and devote the rest of his meaningless life to caring for that ox. He wasn\u8217\'92t going to do that, but he could! He could.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Should he?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The answer was an obvious no, because this was only an ox. Thousands of oxes just like it were being butchered and eaten throughout the world. He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d eaten plenty of ox himself. Helping this ox and not every other ox would make him a hypocrite. There was a whole leech farm full of animals that needed saving down the road, and he hadn\u8217\'92t spared one thought for helping any of them. Were they somehow less worthy than this ox? Was he somehow responsible for the life of every living being in all creation? Where was the line?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He cast a skeptical eye upon the ox as it picked its path around the crooked bean poles. It was clearly too dumb to be a god. And too weak. Poor thing, the whites of its eyes were red from being forced to sniff all that freeweed smoke.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It came to a stop just feet from him. The fluid inside the fat sun blisters on its back continued to swish around.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Damn,\u8221\'94 Mandri muttered.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The beast really needed help.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 But, so did he. And that was the way of the world. Creatures had to take care themselves. There just wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t enough effort to go around.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri sipped his hooch. He held his hand up to bless the ox. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I wish you well,\u8221\'94 he said in regal tones.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took another sip. The burgeonsight had the short yellow hairs on the ox\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s head swirling. He sipped his hooch again and downed the dregs. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Stop looking at me like that,\u8221\'94 he said. \u8220\'93I can\u8217\'92t do anything for you.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Now that he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d said what he said, it could not be taken back.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He made for his tree.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The damned ox followed him.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He sat down in the crook of his tree. The ox sat down nearby\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it lay on its belly like it hadn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t done in over a week. It\u8217\'92s heavy ribs squished a few leeches.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Do we know each other?\u8221\'94 Mandri asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The ox stared back at him, full of bovine dumbness. Its big, dozing breaths rolled its bleeding hide back and forth over its sagging spine. Now that Mandri was positively high on burgeons, the sight of that rolling hide was a sight to see. The skin appeared to boil.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He nestled deeper into the tree trunk\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s embrace as the nightly breeze caressed his cheek. He sighed a little half-sipped, absent sigh, the kind of sigh that he\u8217\'92d make whenever he was ready for a restful sleep. He let his eyelids slouch as he watched flies buzz in and out of ox\u8217\'92s snout. The ox snorted, blowing those fuzzy black dots out of his great big nostrils. The flies zipped right back in to lick at the only reliable source of moisture that they could find in this godforsaken world.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He continued along the road, stomping his way through the muddy wheel-ruts, slapping away the flies, humming nursery rhymes, trying anything to distract himself from the thought that he only had one last , and no real prospects for a client to provide him with more.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t put much stock in philosophies dreamed up while high on burgeons, but in private, eyes closed, he indulged. And who knows? He could see the pattern everywhere: Inhale, Exhale; Hot, Cold; Come, Go. Perhaps the being at the bottom of everything was a pair of lungs, and all of existence shared the same extensive breath, a cosmic exhale motivating all life.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He began to dream about a cosmic inhale, where everything moved, and talked, and breathed in reverse, all the way back to the beginning of time.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And then he crested a hill and saw came upon the leech farm and the shock of it }}